  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The DOReader.java interface is \"for reading Fedora digital objects\" according to the javadoc comment.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "However, it is being used to ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "strong"
        }
       ],
       "text": "modify",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": " objects. See for example line 852 of DefaultManagement.java, which is:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "w.getAuditRecords().add(audit);",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "I believe this is a violation of the DOReader contract.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "A solution would be for SimpleDOReader.getAuditRecords() to return copies instead of the real objects (please note that it will have some impact on DefaultManagement.java at least).",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "As a temporary solution, at least it would be nice to explicitly write in DOReader's javadoc that it is not read-only.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Thanks,",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Keep up the good work,",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Nicolas.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
